From u0c7ffcac30445989 at groups.google.com  Sat Nov 17 01:10:50 2018
From: u0c7ffcac30445989 at groups.google.com (w.cca...@gmail.com)
Date: Sat, 17 Nov 2018 01:10:50 +0000
Subject: hierarchical Poisson regression---top-level posteriors very wide
Message-ID: <Z98HL4t3Nrw.hqsqPie8BwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/Z98HL4t3Nrw/m/hqsqPie8BwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All,

I'm trying to build a simple hierarchical Poisson regression model. I can make it work (i.e., return reasonably narrow regression coefficient posteriors close to true values) for individual models/data, but the top-level posteriors for the regression coefficients are very wide and the means aren't at the true values. It's odd to me b/c in the simulated data (see code below) the number of observations for each model is lower than the number of individual models and, yet, the posteriors for the individual models are good while the posterior describing the top-level distribution (of regression coefficients) is bad. Any ideas?

Code:

poisHRegCode <- nimbleCode({
   if(K > 1){
      A_hat[1:K,1:K] <- inverse(diag(x=rep(1000,K)))
      multimu[1:K] <- rep(0,K)
      B_hat[1:K] ~ dmnorm(mean=multimu[1:K], prec=A_hat[1:K,1:K])
   }
   else{
      B_hat ~ dnorm(mean=0,sd=1000)
   }
   for(m in 1:M){
      if(K > 1){
         A[1:K,1:K] <- inverse(diag(x=rep(1000,K)))
         B[1:K,m] ~ dmnorm(mean=B_hat[1:K], prec=A[1:K,1:K])
      }else{
         B[m] ~ dnorm(mean=B_hat,sd=1000)
      }
   }
   for (m in 1:M){
      for (n in 1:N){
         if(K > 1){
            lambda[n,m] <- inprod(X[n,1:K,m], B[1:K,m])
         }else{
            lambda[n,m] <- X[n,m] * B[m]
         }
         Y[n,m] ~ dpois(exp(lambda[n,m]))
      }
   }
})

N <- 6
M <- 7
X_list <- lapply(1:M,function(x)c(rep(1,10),rnorm(10)))
X <- array(unlist(X_list),dim=c(N,2,M))
Bs <- rnorm(n=M,mean=0.5,sd=0.1)
L <- c()
for(j in 1:length(Bs)){
   L <- cbind(L,apply(X[,,j],1,function(k,b)return(k[1] + b * k[2]),b=Bs[j]))
}
Y <- apply(L,2,function(l)sapply(l,function(j)rpois(lambda=exp(j),n=1)))
K <- 2

poisHRegData <- list(Y=Y,
                  X=X)

poisHRegConsts <- list(N=N,
                     K=K,
                     M=M)

poisHRegInits <- list(B_hat=rep(0,K))

poisHRegModel <- nimbleModel(code=poisHRegCode,
                        data=poisHRegData,
                        inits=poisHRegInits,
                        constants=poisHRegConsts)

#compile nimble model to C++ code—much faster runtime
C_poisHRegModel <- compileNimble(poisHRegModel, showCompilerOutput = FALSE)

#configure the MCMC
poisHRegModel_conf <- configureMCMC(poisHRegModel)

#add monitors
poisHRegModel_conf$addMonitors(c("B"))

#build MCMC
poisHRegModelMCMC <- buildMCMC(poisHRegModel_conf,thin=1,enableWAIC = TRUE)

#compile MCMC to C++—much faster
C_poisHRegModelMCMC <- compileNimble(poisHRegModelMCMC,project=poisHRegModel)

#number of MCMC iterations
niter=10000

#set seed for replicability
set.seed(1)

#call the C++ compiled MCMC model
C_poisHRegModelMCMC$run(niter)

#save the MCMC chain (monitored variables) as a matrix
samples <- as.matrix(C_poisHRegModelMCMC$mvSamples)

WAIC <- C_poisHRegModelMCMC$calculateWAIC(nburnin=1000)

From u5fa2a363d4ddd1d1 at groups.google.com  Sat Nov 17 21:46:03 2018
From: u5fa2a363d4ddd1d1 at groups.google.com (Chris Paciorek)
Date: Sat, 17 Nov 2018 21:46:03 +0000
Subject: Re: hierarchical Poisson regression---top-level posteriors very wide
Message-ID: <Z98HL4t3Nrw.JZ1aQ5FuBwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Z98HL4t3Nrw.hqsqPie8BwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
References: <Z98HL4t3Nrw.hqsqPie8BwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/Z98HL4t3Nrw/m/JZ1aQ5FuBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Chris, this doesn't look like anything related to NIMBLE but rather
with your model structure.

Considering just your K=1 case (the K>1 should be similar)...

When you have:
B[m] ~ dnorm(mean=B_hat,sd=1000)

you're basically saying that the B[m] values can be very far from
B_hat. Given this wide variance, you can't learn much about B_hat.
Even if your data could pin down each B[m] without any uncertainty,
you only have 7 B[m] values to try to learn about B_hat in a situation
where you say with certainty that the signal to noise ratio is very
low (sd=1000).

Given the normality here, we know analytically that the posterior
variance for B_hat is 1 / (1/1000^2 + 7/1000^2) = 125000 (e.g., see
Gelman et al Bayesian data analysis book, section 2.5).

The standard model structure here would be to have something like:
B[m] ~ dnorm(mean=B_hat,sd=tau)
and put a prior on tau  (e.g., tau ~ dunif(0, some_big_value)) or a
half-Cauchy distribution). That way you can learn how close the B[m]
values are to the B_hat based on the data.  And then, presuming that
tau is not estimated to be too large, you'll be able to learn about
B_hat from what you can learn about the B[m] values.

Side note: you don't really need inverse(). You can just do:
A_hat <- diag(x=rep(1/1000,K))
And in fact since A_hat is constant, you could just initialize it
outside the model and pass it in as either 'inits' or 'constants'.
Doesn't matter much with small K and because A_hat is never updated in
the MCMC, but just a note.

Second side note: I would not initialize A inside the loop, since it
is not indexed by 'm'. The result is that you are asking nimble to
create the same node multiple times. Doesn't cause a problem here, but
it could lead to problems in other situations.

Chris

